  "meta": {
    "title": "aⁱbʲcᵏ where i = j or j = k",
    "blurb": "The first move is a guess: check that the a’s match the b’s, or that the b’s match the c’s. The textbook inherently ambiguous language — every grammar for it has two parse trees for aⁿbⁿcⁿ, and no deterministic PDA recognises it.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε"
      },
      {
        "w": "abcc",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "i = j"
      },
      {
        "w": "cc",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "i = j = 0"
      },
      {
        "w": "abbcc",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "j = k"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabbcc",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "both"
      },
      {
        "w": "abbc",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabbbc",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "acb",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "ambiguity",
        "nondeterminism",
        "textbook"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "chapter": "Sipser, Introduction to the Theory of Computation, §2.1",
      "readme": "Two machines behind one guess. The upper branch pushes an A per a and pops one per b, then ignores the c's. The lower branch ignores the a's, pushes a B per b and pops one per c. A word is accepted when either branch reaches its accepting state with the input used up.\n\nWords of the form **aⁿbⁿcⁿ** are accepted by **both** branches, and that is the point. Any context-free grammar for this language must, for all large enough n, give those words two different parse trees — the language is *inherently ambiguous*, the classic example of it. A single stack cannot check both equalities at once; that would be aⁿbⁿcⁿ, which is not context-free."
    }
  }
}
